---
import { Image } from "astro:assets";
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import Excerpt from "../components/Excerpt.astro";
import Foundation from "../components/Foundation.astro";
import WaitlistForm from "../components/WaitlistForm.astro";
import logo from "../assets/brand/logo-lockup.png";
import { homeExcerpt } from "../data/excerpts";
import { site } from "../data/site";
---

<Base title={site.name} description={site.description}>
  <section class="hero wrap">
    <p class="kicker">Self-paced · Greece, Rome, the Renaissance</p>
    <h1 class="hero-logo">
      <Image
        src={logo}
        alt="Sophandros"
        width={923}
        height={524}
        class="hero-logo-img"
        loading="eager"
      />
    </h1>
    <p class="hero-promise">A modern revival of the classical education.</p>
    <div class="actions">
      <a class="button" href="/lessons/the-birth-of-civilization">Start with the prologue</a>
      <a class="button ghost" href="/curriculum">Curriculum</a>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap-narrow">
      <div class="section-head">
        <span class="kicker">What it is</span>
        <h2>The progression</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          A classical education was a way of watching human ideals change, and of
          watching the inventions and paradigm shifts that followed them. You see
          why a form prospered and why it failed, and you come to understand why
          we do what we do today. The American Founders used these books that way
          when they argued about a republic: what to take from the old
          constitutions, and what to change. Sophandros revives that education for a
          working life now.
        </p>
        <p>
          The reading keeps the old order. Greece builds one figure, layer by
          layer, as that society grows more complex. You start with honor: the
          warrior, in the Bronze Age world of the <em>Iliad</em>. Then a mind and
          a home. Then a citizen who also fights. Then a conscience and a law.
          Then a mind that examines itself. Then practical wisdom, with Aristotle
          and the Parthenon.
        </p>
        <p>
          Rome carries that person into duty and law. The Renaissance carries him
          into the courtier, and into the professional you already are. Each
          lesson is on this page, and the primary text is in the reader beside it.
          The shorter path through the founding is the
          <a href="/founders">Founders Edition</a>.
        </p>
      </div>
      <Foundation />
      <Excerpt excerpt={homeExcerpt} />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap-narrow">
      <div class="section-head">
        <span class="kicker">Who it is for</span>
        <h2>People whose weeks are already full</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          It is for a professional who wants the sequence behind the work itself:
          why a practice, a law, or a building looks the way it does. The course
          was shaped around a physician in training, someone who can give the work
          a few hours, set it down, and pick it up again. Several of the Greece
          questions ask what a text has to say to medicine, and to any craft that
          judges particular cases.
        </p>
        <p>
          You want Homer, Plato, Virgil, and Palladio, and enough Latin and Greek to
          meet a sentence in the original. The stated aim is modest. By the end you
          should be able to read adapted Caesar and Cicero, and Virgil with notes.
          Full fluency is not the promise.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="section-head">
        <span class="kicker">The arc</span>
        <h2>Three phases</h2>
      </div>
      <p class="prose">
        Four parts. Greece is the ideals, from a warrior’s honor to practical
        wisdom, and the prologue and the first chapter can be read now. Rome is
        duty and law. The Renaissance is what a later age made of that inheritance.
        The Founding is the culmination. The later lessons are still to be written.
      </p>
      <div class="phases">
        <article class="phase">
          <span class="phase-num">I</span>
          <h3>Greece</h3>
          <p class="meta-line">About twelve months · Drafted</p>
          <p>
            Six layers, from the warrior’s honor to practical wisdom. Homer through
            Aristotle and the Parthenon.
          </p>
          <a class="more" href="/curriculum#greece">Open the curriculum</a>
        </article>
        <article class="phase">
          <span class="phase-num">II</span>
          <h3>Rome</h3>
          <p class="meta-line">About twelve months · Coming soon</p>
          <p>
            The same person, carried into duty and law: Livy, Cicero, the
            <em>Aeneid</em>, Seneca, Marcus Aurelius, Vitruvius.
          </p>
          <a class="more" href="/curriculum#rome">Open the curriculum</a>
        </article>
        <article class="phase">
          <span class="phase-num">III</span>
          <h3>Renaissance</h3>
          <p class="meta-line">About twelve months · Coming soon</p>
          <p>
            Then the courtier, and the modern professional: Petrarch to Palladio,
            Ascham, Milton, and Montaigne.
          </p>
          <a class="more" href="/curriculum#renaissance">Open the curriculum</a>
        </article>
        <article class="phase">
          <span class="phase-num">IV</span>
          <h3>The Founding</h3>
          <p class="meta-line">Coming soon</p>
          <p>
            The culmination: the nation, looking to the world. Greece, Rome, and
            the Renaissance carried into a new republic.
          </p>
          <a class="more" href="/curriculum#founding">Open the curriculum</a>
        </article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" id="how-it-works">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="section-head">
        <span class="kicker">How it works</span>
        <h2>Four steps, on this site</h2>
      </div>
      <p class="prose">
        A lesson takes about half an hour. You are not sent out to buy the book
        first. The text is already on the page.
      </p>
      <ol class="how-steps">
        <li>
          <span>1</span>
          <h3>Read the lesson here</h3>
          <p>A short essay tells you the context, the ideas, and why they matter.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <span>2</span>
          <h3>Study the primary text in the reader</h3>
          <p>The public-domain translation is on the same page, with a note on the hard words.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <span>3</span>
          <h3>Practice the Latin or the Greek</h3>
          <p>One word, and a line to say aloud. A grammar book is optional.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <span>4</span>
          <h3>Check yourself</h3>
          <p>A few questions with the answers revealed, then a short reflection.</p>
        </li>
      </ol>
      <p class="how-cta">
        <a class="button" href="/lessons/the-birth-of-civilization">Start with the prologue</a>
      </p>

    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap-narrow">
      <div class="section-head">
        <span class="kicker">The languages</span>
        <h2>One word, on the lesson</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          Each lesson gives you a Greek or Latin word from the reading, and a line
          to say aloud. That is the practice. You can finish Lesson 1 with nothing
          but this site.
        </p>
        <p>
          A grammar book is a way to go further, not a requirement. If you want one
          later: Wheelock’s <em>Latin</em> is grammar first, and Ørberg’s
          <em>Lingua Latina per se Illustrata</em> is reading first. Greek, when you
          come to it, can use <em>Athenaze</em>. None of those books is how you start.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap-narrow">
      <div class="section-head">
        <span class="kicker">Optional</span>
        <h2>A tutor, a cohort, a journey</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          The lesson stands on its own. A grammar book, a double translation in the
          manner of Ascham’s <em>Scholemaster</em> (1570), and a tutor’s review of
          that translation are later extras. So are the three things below.
        </p>
        <blockquote class="quote">
          Not to learn Principles but to enlarge Experience.
          <footer>John Milton, <cite>Of Education</cite> (1644), on seeing other countries</footer>
        </blockquote>
        <p>
          Milton wants a journey to enlarge experience, after the reading. If you
          take one, it closes a phase. It is not how a lesson begins.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>A tutor.</strong> Thirty to forty-five minutes a month, or every
          fortnight, with a classicist who marks translations and can write a
          quarterly note.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>A cohort.</strong> A live monthly seminar of eight to twelve, on
          the text of the module.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Travel, as a capstone.</strong> A short Athens leg after Greece.
          Rome and Naples after Rome. Then, if you wish, a Giro of fourteen to
          twenty-one days through Florence, Rome, the Veneto, and Venice, with an
          established operator. None of it is required to finish the course.
        </p>
        <p>
          There is also a shorter outline, the
          <a href="/founders">Founders Edition</a>: twelve weeks on the Greek and
          Roman reading behind the American republic. And an elective still unwritten,
          Physic: Hippocrates, Galen, and Vesalius.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section band" id="waitlist">
    <div class="wrap-narrow">
      <div class="section-head">
        <span class="kicker">The list</span>
        <h2>Ask to be told when it opens</h2>
      </div>
      <p class="prose">
        Enrollment is not open. Pricing has not been set. Leave an address if you
        want word when the first places are offered.
      </p>
      <WaitlistForm id="home-waitlist" compact />
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
